Date: Wed, 3 Feb 93 11:51:08 PST
From: xanadu!tribble@uunet.UU.NET (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

All the descriptions of why teh futures market is zero-sum have
neglected a major component of why people get into the futures market:
risk management.  

The contract:

	One year from now, Hal will deliver one ounce of gold to Robin in
	exchange for $450.

In Hal's example futures contract, one of them pays off the other if
the future value of gold is different.  That may be zero-sum.  But....

What if Robin knows he needs gold in the future, and he has to be able
budget for a maximum price.  He's not trying to make money based on
his expectations of the price of gold (the zero-sum game), he's trying
to control his uncertainty so that some long-term plan makes sense.
That might have a lot of value to him; far more than the transaction
cost and opportunity cost if gold takes an advantageous price plunge
in a year.

Likewise, Hal might not have a clue about the price of gold, but might
know that some will become available to him in a year.  He'd like to
lock in a price now so that he knows that gold will pay his mortgage.
Without futures, the gold that he will acquire is a wild card.  With
futures, he can lock in the value now, and so plan much more
effectively.

This obviously scales to things like supermarkets and orange juice.
Much of the value in futures markets is for hedging and controlling
(and trading) uncertainty.  That's where the game stops being merely
zero-sum.  

Note that Robin's idea-futures can serve the same purpose for ideas.

dean

