Subject: Insurance fraud

   Perry E. Metzger writes: 
   >Insurance fraud, for instance, is suprisingly rare -- this because
   >insurance companies are good at combatting it and fraudsters have
   >little public support.

In some places in Massachusetts, auto insurance fraud is a real
problem.  For instance, in one city in Mass., the average two-car
accident involves 5 passengers.

The reason for this is a classic case of the absence of market
feedback: A large fraction of the autos in Mass. are in the "assigned
risk pool", which here means that an insurance company is assigned to
handle the paperwork, claims adjusting, etc., but that the actual risk
is borne by an insurance pool run by the state.  Since the
investigating insurance company has no incentive to detect fraud,
fraud proliferates.

