Subject: PPL: Iceland

   From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)

   If the question revolves around how to do something within a
   simply-connected voluntarist community, then the Icelandic experience
   may be a good guide; but they never had more than one legal code at a
   time, so any question of what institutions might evolve in a world
   with a non-trivial free market in law can't be solved by appeal to
   Iceland.

Iceland is an interesting example...  People seem to be holding it up
as an example of "private law", a PPL, but for some reason, it leaves
me cold.  I can't exactly explain why, but it seems much more like the
"customary law" of pre-state-level societies than a voluntary, binding
contractual obligation undertaken by a bunch of otherwise autonomous
individuals.  I'm sure that this can be argued both ways, but I think
the following bears scruitiny: to the extent that the decisions of the
Althing were practically binding on anyone, they were binding on
everyone.  (There was no special class of people who were recognized
to have "opted out" of the Althing, and who had a greater practical
ability to defy the Althing than anyone else did.)

